<div id="S4.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Generalizability of DAC-decoder.</em>We replace the original decoders of CLIP-Count <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and VLCounter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> with our DAC-decoder in Tab.<a href="#S4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 4.3 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Text-promptable Object Counting via Quantity Awareness Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. Both outperform their original versions, showcasing the generalizability of DAC-decoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Loss functions</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Vision-text quantity alignment loss <math id="S4.SS3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{align}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{align}</annotation></semantics></math>.</em>
To understand the contribution of each term in the alignment loss <math id="S4.SS3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{align}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{align}</annotation></semantics></math>, we present two variants by removing the first or second term from Eqn.<a href="#S3.Ex1" title="In 3.4 Network optimization ‣ 3 Method ‣ Text-promptable Object Counting via Quantity Awareness Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> to investigate the effectiveness of both, namely QUANet(<math id="S4.SS3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{align}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{align}</annotation></semantics></math> w/o FT/ST) in Tab. <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.3 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Text-promptable Object Counting via Quantity Awareness Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. Both leads to performance drop, attesting their effectiveness.
The first term <math id="S4.SS3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{N}f(s^{n}_{i}-s^{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{N}f(s^{n}_{i}-s^{p})</annotation></semantics></math> essentially plays the same role to a standard vision–text contrastive loss as in <math id="S4.SS3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{vtc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{vtc}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, which treats all negative samples equally. We found their effects to be empirically similar: using only the first term (QUANet(<math id="S4.SS3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{align}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{align}</annotation></semantics></math> w/o ST)) yields similar (slightly better) results to replacing <math id="S4.SS3.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{align}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{align}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{vtc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{vtc}</annotation></semantics></math> (QUANet(<math id="S4.SS3.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{align}\rightarrow L_{vtc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{align}\rightarrow L_{vtc}</annotation></semantics></math>)).
Next, the second term enforces a ranking among the similarity scores of those negative vision-text pairs. This quantity-aware refinement offers clear improvement in the overall performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS3.p17.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cross-stream quantity ranking loss <math id="S4.SS3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{rank}</annotation></semantics></math>.</em> This loss is proposed to optimize patch-level quantity ranks both within and across the predictions of the two streams in the DAC-decoder. As shown in Tab. <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.3 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Text-promptable Object Counting via Quantity Awareness Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, omitting <math id="S4.SS3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{rank}</annotation></semantics></math> leads to deteriorated results on both datasets, <em id="S4.SS3.p17.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS3.p17.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, the MAE increases by +1.70 on the validation set and +1.81 on the test set. This demonstrates the superiority of <math id="S4.SS3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{rank}</annotation></semantics></math> in reducing counting errors.
Furthermore, we introduce a variant of <math id="S4.SS3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{rank}</annotation></semantics></math> in which the patch-level ranking constraints are applied solely within each independent stream of DAC-decoder. This variant, denoted by QUANet(<math id="S4.SS3.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{rank}\rightarrow L_{srank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{rank}\rightarrow L_{srank}</annotation></semantics></math>) in Tab. <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.3 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Text-promptable Object Counting via Quantity Awareness Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, leads to a performance decline, with MAE increased by +1.12 on the test set. We also experiment with maintaining only the cross-stream ranking constraint, omitting the ranking constraint within each stream. This variant is denoted as QUANet(<math id="S4.SS3.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{rank}\rightarrow L_{crank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{rank}\rightarrow L_{crank}</annotation></semantics></math>) in Tab. <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.3 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Text-promptable Object Counting via Quantity Awareness Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, and it is also inferior to the original <math id="S4.SS3.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{rank}</annotation></semantics></math>. Last, we vary the patch interval <math id="S4.SS3.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS3.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{rank}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eqn.<a href="#S3.Ex2" title="In 3.4 Network optimization ‣ 3 Method ‣ Text-promptable Object Counting via Quantity Awareness Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) from 1 to 9. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Text-promptable Object Counting via Quantity Awareness Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b), QUANet performs the best when <math id="S4.SS3.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=5</annotation></semantics></math>, which is our default setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Loss weight between <math id="S4.SS3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{align}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{align}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{rank}</annotation></semantics></math>.</em> We investigate the influence of the loss weight <math id="S4.SS3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> in Eqn. <a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 3.4 Network optimization ‣ 3 Method ‣ Text-promptable Object Counting via Quantity Awareness Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Text-promptable Object Counting via Quantity Awareness Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c), our QUANet performs the best when <math id="S4.SS3.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> on the validation set (default), and concurrently also the best on the test set.</p>
</div>
