</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Additional analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complexity.</span> We compare the parameters and inference speed between QUANet and current methods  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. As shown in Tab. <a href="#S4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 4.4 Additional analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Text-promptable Object Counting via Quantity Awareness Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, QUANet shows comparable complexity to others.
Notice 1) DAVE<sub id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prm</span></sub> and VA-Count are multi-stage works, <em id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span> VA-Count employs GroundingDINO, CLIP, <em id="S4.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">etc</em>.<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text"></span>, we can only roughly estimate their complexities; 2) the DAC-decoder of QUANet account for less than 10% of the total parameters.</p>
</div>
